Copilot Readiness Impact
The tenant's Copilot readiness score sits at 55 out of 100, against a Gate threshold of 82 — a gap of 27 points that the tenant has not yet cleared. The licensing pillar's score of 54 reflects affordability posture rather than safety posture; with 1 paid seat provisioned and 0 unassigned, there is simply no Copilot licence present in this tenant, which is recorded as the critical finding and is the direct reason the readiness score cannot advance from its current position.

Licensing's role here is narrow but consequential in one specific direction: no Copilot licence means no rollout is possible yet, regardless of how the remaining readiness pillars resolve. The warning finding compounds this — 1 of 93 scanned SharePoint sites is shared with Everyone except external users, meaning every internal user already holds standing access to that content. Because Copilot inherits permissions rather than intent and grounds its responses on whatever content a given user can already reach, any future Copilot-enabled user would be able to surface material from that site without any additional access step being taken.

The practical consequence is that the data-exposure condition exists independently of licensing and will remain in place whether or not a licence is introduced — resolving the licence gap first, without also narrowing the sharing scope, does not reduce the risk the warning describes.

